WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:15.740
[Drumming]

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.260
Yellow fever has been the scourge of Africa
since the 15th century,

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.741
according to oral accounts of early epidemics.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.960
This virus disease is transmitted by mosquitoes.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:37.899
Man, other primates, and some mammals are
the reservoir.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:45.400
Historically, yellow fever has remained endemic in the rain forest zone favorable to its maintenance cycle

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.940
between the latitudes of fifteen degrees
north and fifteen degrees south.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:55.650
Yet, as of now, this zone is still expanding
south into arid habitats.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.140
In southern Sudan, the Nuba Mountains are home to a population which,

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:07.740
despite its geographic and social isolation, has been repeatedly visited by the virus since 1934.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:13.450
The largest-ever epidemic occurred in 1940
with 20,000 cases reported

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.880
and a mortality of up to 20 percent.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.120
The disease became endemic and kept creeping south.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:29.840
At the Naval Medical Research Unit-3 in Cairo, Egypt, scientists from the Rockefeller Foundation

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.790
were actively engaged in the study of West
Nile and other arboviruses.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:39.510
It was thus decided to undertake a reconnaissance in southern Sudan.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:47.110
The objective was twofold: determine if there had been yellow fever transmission subsequent to 1940,

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.880
and then find out if the galago, a primate common in tropical Africa,

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.520
plays a role as a reservoir south of the recognized endemic zone.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:02.260
The opportunity for such investigation was
provided by Dr. Mansour,

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:08.260
professor of microbiology at the School of Medicine in Khartoum and the Sudan Veterinary Services.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.260
They were undertaking the extermination of all warm-blooded animals in an attempt

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:20.740
to eradicate the tsetse fly south of the Bahr
el Arab by eliminating the source of blood meals.

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:29.790
The fly, which transmitts trypanosomiasis,
also known as sleeping sickness,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.610
had been responsible for a high mortality in cattle.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:39.760
As a result, whole populations of Nilotic,
indigenous people fled southern Sudan.

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:45.269
By interrupting the breeding cycle, it was hoped that people would return to the area and raise cattle,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.440
a source of beef which could be exported
via Khartoum and Port Sudan.

00:02:52.719 --> 00:02:58.739
The NAMRU team went along with the veterinarian
teams in charge of eliminating wild animals,

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:04.939
and canvassed the area from El Obeid to Gogrial, making blood collections from humans

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.620
and various species of primates. Eleven villages were visited.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:18.950
As the DC progresses up the Nile, a golden
mosaic of canyons and twisted earth unfolds.

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:24.389
This is the Nubian Desert, a mystical palette of ochres and shadows

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.700
transfixed by the abysmal gorge of the river.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:36.840
The perpetuity of these arid horizons seems to float in a reddish haze of light, heat, and dust.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:43.180
Much of this is now under the waters of Lake Nasser, because the Aswan Dam was built in the sixties.

00:03:47.359 --> 00:03:52.379
The city of Khartoum was built in 1821 by
the Egyptian army.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:59.840
Its name means "elephant trunk" because here the Blue Nile concludes its tumultuous descent

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:07.100
from the highlandsof Ethiopia and meets in a sweeping curve,  the White Nile flowing from Uganda.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:12.379
Adjacent to the desert are the irrigated farms of the people who live on the banks of the Nile.

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:24.159
The confluence takes place under the bridge linking Khartoum to the industrial hub of Omdurman.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.370
The color contrast is noteworthy.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:45.410
These ancient falouccas are still the main
means of transport along and across the river.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:59.620
Lord Kitchener relieved the British forces led by Gordon and besieged in Khartoum for ten months by the Mahdi.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:10.370
Chinese Gordon, so nicknamed because of his heroic Crimean campaigns,

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.820
remained defiant until the final assault and was killed.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:19.720
Both statues were removed when Sudan gained its independence.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.540
The tomb of the Mahdi is on the other side
of the river in Omdurman.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.640
It is a holy place of pilgrimage for the Moslem Sudanese.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:35.130
The blue cupola is topped by the Moslem crescent penetrated by a spear

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.620
which is the emblem of the Mahdi himself.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:47.360
In front of the laboratory, equipment is loaded onto a Jeep.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.740
It is loaned by the U.S. Map Service.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.810
They are busy mapping the continent of Africa from Cape to Cairo

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:58.330
in preparation for the first space flight around the world.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:12.150
Wherever the British arrived, they developed the railway network, and such was the case for Sudan.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:17.960
Khartoum became the hub and a transport point for the Red Sea and Port Sudan.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:25.530
Two other spurs went respectively north to
Darfur and south to Kordofan and El Obeid.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:29.960
All heavy materiel must be hoisted on a flatbed.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:35.940
This Electrolux refrigerator is operated with
kerosene and will be indispensable

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:42.120
for storage of field-collected specimens.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.770
The microbiology department involved in tropical disease research

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:53.050
has utilized the railroad system for transporting a laboratory car to various parts of Sudan.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:59.660
Personal effects and more fragile equipment are loaded in this car, which will be pooled at the end of the railway.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:06.040
The tarpaulin will provide shelter when specimens are being brought from the bush.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:12.250
This magnificent steam locomotive will reach El Obeid at the top speed of 35 miles an hour,

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:16.660
and there will be only a few places to fill up the water tank.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:26.210
The engineers are putting the last nurturing
touches of oil. Recommendations are made.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:29.310
Everything is checked to ensure a safe voyage.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:39.800
The two hundred and fifty mile train journey ends at El Obeid in central Sudan.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:45.310
This is a road and caravan junction on the
migration paths from west Africa.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:51.080
This is also the end of the railroad and from there the two thousand mile inland trek

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:58.080
to the Nuba Mountains will be on a dirt and sand road, too hard or too soft for driving comfort.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:12.520
[Music]

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:19.900
Short grass savannah, thorny bushes, acacias
are the main features of the Kordofan province.

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:26.900
As the train stops to take on water, the steam
is converted to hot water for tea.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:42.080
This truck, taking an audacious turn, is heavily loaded with equipment and personal baggage from the laboratory car.

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:47.920
It also carries the field assistants.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:52.640
Kasha is the first Nuba village to be reached.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:57.480
Its round huts gripping the flanks of a volcanic outcrop are typical.

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.260
Such hillocks cover approximately three thousand square miles

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:06.640
and range from three hundred to thirty-six hundred feet in height.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:15.639
Women are returning to the village carrying water collected from far away in large gourds.

00:09:15.639 --> 00:09:19.859
Their elongated silhouettes are astonishingly regal and beautiful.

00:09:23.709 --> 00:09:28.600
The Nuba people have been victims of Arab
slave traders for centuries and have learned

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:34.280
how to protect themselves in these tiny villages,
tucked among forbidding cliffs and peaks.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:37.600
Water comes from springs and percolation pockets.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:43.160
Some tribes have remained isolated from each other with little or no social interaction.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:50.160
They speak close to a hundred different dialects.
The Nuba are essentially farmers.

00:09:50.329 --> 00:09:54.509
They are animists and worship their cattle,
which they believe harbor the ghost

00:09:54.660 --> 00:10:00.140
of their ancestor; therefore, it will be rarely sacrificed for food.

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:09.699
These are some of the boys from whom it would
be desirable to obtain a blood sample to determine

00:10:09.699 --> 00:10:16.699
whether they have antibodies to yellow fever
which would reflect transmission since 1940.

00:10:18.269 --> 00:10:24.220
To befriend the population is a task greatly
facilitated by the presence of Dr. Mansour

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:30.040
who is Sudanese and speaks the language.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:36.089
These young warriors have sophisticated mohawks
which accentuate the beauty of their profiles.

00:10:36.089 --> 00:10:41.740
They often wear on their chest a leather pouch
which contains a few verses of the Koran.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:43.839
This is for good luck.

00:10:43.839 --> 00:10:50.839
Every man carries a spear which is used mostly
for cultivating but also for hunting.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:59.920
Both men and women undergo painful scarifications
for cosmetic purposes.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:05.300
It is also a test of courage and stoicism
for the young adults. Skins incisions are

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:10.100
made with sharp knives, thorns or pieces of
glass rubbed with dirt.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:15.790
hese wounds are left to fester producing the
raised scar known as keloids.

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:22.790
In women, elaborate patterns are added for
each major life event, such as puberty, marriage,

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:30.360
and the birth of children. [Music]

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:45.350
In Talodi, word is passed onto the chief without whom nothing could be accomplished.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:01.860
A suitable explanation must be provided in order to bleed the population.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:06.619
Dr. Taylor is an old hand at yellow fever,
having been the director of the yellow fever

00:12:06.619 --> 00:12:13.129
laboratory in Rio de Janeiro and ultimately,
at the Rockefeller Institute in New York.

00:12:13.129 --> 00:12:20.059
He is an expert at coercing smiles from these
young children. The operation is supervised

00:12:20.059 --> 00:12:26.160
with great interest. Four children, twelve
and thirteen years old, tested positive in

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.790
the foothills of the Nuba Mountains.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:35.790
This demonstrated that yellow fever transmission
had occurred after 1940.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:43.379
This little primate with globulous eyes and
long fingers is a Galago or bush baby. It

00:12:43.379 --> 00:12:47.589
is nocturnal and sleeps during the day in
tree hollows.

00:12:47.589 --> 00:12:54.589
Local boys will capture them at night by harassing
them in the trees where they are feeding.

00:12:55.709 --> 00:13:01.259
The hussar monkey is so named after his red
coat and because he marches in open spaces

00:13:01.259 --> 00:13:08.259
with military precision. He is one of the
less common primates of tropical Africa.

00:13:10.779 --> 00:13:17.100
The Baggara or cattlemen are nomadic decendents
of 14th century Bedouin invaders.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:23.829
A baobab tree breaks the monotony of their
journey. It is home to many animals, and people

00:13:23.829 --> 00:13:26.389
store water inside its trunk.

00:13:26.389 --> 00:13:33.389
The pulp of its fruit is commonly used to
treat diarrhea.

00:13:37.519 --> 00:13:43.160
The Baggara migrate from western Africa during
the dry season. They congregate around the

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:49.929
depressions called Wadi where water accumulates
all year round. What role these people play

00:13:49.929 --> 00:13:56.929
in transport of yellow fever is unknown.

00:14:01.929 --> 00:14:07.689
This large herd of cattle has traveled nearly
one thousand miles and it represents many

00:14:07.689 --> 00:14:14.619
breeds. The black Zebu from India with a typical
hump has large round white horns.

00:14:14.619 --> 00:14:21.619
The Ankoli-Watusi is a mix of Zebu and a local
African breed. Its large extended horns are

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:29.610
reminiscent of the Texas longhorn.

00:14:34.529 --> 00:14:40.329
This is the home of the Dinka who belong to
the River Lake Nilotes, the largest ethnic

00:14:40.329 --> 00:14:47.329
group in southern Sudan. Tending goats, sheep,
and cattle is their primary activity.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:15:00.540
In this area, water is extracted from fifteen
to twenty feet deep wells and transported

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:07.540
for storage into large troughs. Branches and
leaves are used as a filter.

00:15:15.339 --> 00:15:21.639
Mosquito larvae can be found on the surface.
There was no time to investigate these mosquitoes,

00:15:21.639 --> 00:15:28.639
which are important vectors of jungle yellow
fever.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:42.889
For the Baggara, the ox is a common means
of transportation and replaces the horse which

00:15:42.889 --> 00:15:47.769
would not survive in this ecology because
of the tsetse fly- transmitted diseases.

00:15:47.769 --> 00:15:54.559
Again, everyone carries a spear for protection
from lions but also from cattle raiders and

00:15:54.559 --> 00:16:01.559
other possible rogues. The procession meanders
through in the savannah interspersed with

00:16:07.189 --> 00:16:14.189
the cautious. [Music]

00:16:15.199 --> 00:16:20.649
These guinea fowl are scampering when the
truck appears. This is probably the most common

00:16:20.649 --> 00:16:27.649
animal of the area and it provides the meat
that could not be carried on the trip.

00:16:29.369 --> 00:16:36.029
This Cercopithecus Aethiops is a common resident
of the jungle. A blood sample will be obtained

00:16:36.029 --> 00:16:37.920
during its necropsy.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:43.029
It is possible to draw about sixty milliliters
from the heart of warm-blooded animals up

00:16:43.029 --> 00:16:46.410
to two hours after death.

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:51.610
Specimens collected in the first fifteen days
of this investigation were driven to Wau on

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:56.610
ice for air shipment to Khartoum and proper
storage until the return to Egypt.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:02.819
A total of one hundred and ten primates were collected.

00:17:02.819 --> 00:17:08.980
Seventy to eighty percent of the grivets and ninety percent of the baboons
had antibody to yellow fever.

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:15.870
The number of positive galagos and red hussar
was below six percent regardless of the area,

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:22.870
therefore, these two primates were not a significant
virus reservoir.

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:28.769
The Bar el Arab is very low because of the
lateness of the dry season, but it still requires

00:17:28.769 --> 00:17:35.299
a ferry to reach the Mayem Mission. It is
the most southern point of this reconnaisance.

00:17:35.299 --> 00:17:42.299
Here the rainfall can reach forty inches as
compared to thirteen in northern Sudan. Lakes,

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:49.600
water holes, and marshes abound.

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:06.490
The curiosity of the Dinka people does not
abate as the Jeep makes its way to the mission.

00:18:09.789 --> 00:18:15.250
Our knowledge about the role played by wild
birds in circulating many viruses transmitted

00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:21.500
by mosquitoes and ticks has accumulated for
fifty years since the inception of the Rockefeller

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:22.929
program.

00:18:22.929 --> 00:18:29.929
Here are ibis, stilts, and egrets feeding
togther. Egrets, of course, are involved in

00:18:30.059 --> 00:18:37.059
the Japanese B-West Nile virus complex scenario
which was to unfold in Egypt, and later on

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:42.149
was to be recognized in Asia.

00:18:42.149 --> 00:18:48.630
The whale head stork, also known as shoebill
stork, has a melancholic pause for the photographer.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:51.740
It beauty is debatable.

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:58.740
It possesses an enormous bill with a hook
to dig for lungfish, frogs, and other delicacies.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:08.000
Two crested cranes are parading their colorful elegance.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:17.220
They are considered sacred and can only belong to kings.

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:26.390
These giraffes have escaped the veterinary
hunters. This is baboon country.

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:32.730
These flooded areas constitute the Sudd from which the name Sudan comes from.

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:35.380
It is a band one hundred miles long.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:41.149
It becomes inundated during the rainy season,
as the water flows from Lake Victoria and

00:19:41.149 --> 00:19:44.399
into the Nile, becoming a regular river.

00:19:44.399 --> 00:19:51.340
It will subsequently reach the Mediterranean,
four thousand miles further north. Thompson

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:58.090
gazelles appear at sunset. Here is the residue
of inundated marshlands.

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:03.260
It's a mecca for all kinds of water birds
who spend a good deal of their life feeding

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:10.000
on the fish and other life essential to their
survival.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:15.360
When the animals are killed, their skin is
removed, saturated with insecticides, and

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:19.399
put on a frame to attract the tsetse flies.

00:20:19.399 --> 00:20:25.840
They will die upon feeding on this poison.
This is another strategy to break the tripanosome

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:32.840
cycle of transmission.

00:20:34.519 --> 00:20:40.779
Behind these Dinka are termite mounds. The
material extracted from these mounds becomes

00:20:40.779 --> 00:20:46.549
very adhesive when wet and can be used to
build houses.

00:20:46.549 --> 00:20:53.549
Two marabout storks comtemplate their domain.
They have a pompous gait which gave them the

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:58.370
nickname of adjutant storks.

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:05.370
Their formidable bill makes them a successful
and rarely-challenged scavenger even for these

00:21:06.730 --> 00:21:11.760
vultures which abound in the region.

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:18.760
A new village is being established. The building
of these houses is undertaken by men only.

00:21:21.130 --> 00:21:24.480
Some are loosening the dirt from termites'
mounds.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:30.909
Others are mixing it with sand in a water
pit. The chief is supervising. A very well

00:21:30.909 --> 00:21:37.279
dressed Dinka wears three strings of beads,
otherwise, these people, well adapted to the

00:21:37.279 --> 00:21:40.940
hot climate of Sudan, wear no clothes.

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:45.889
This social affair is carried at a leisurely
pace.

00:21:45.889 --> 00:21:52.889
A dragon lizard is worried by all this effervescence.
He might lose his abode. [Clapping]

00:22:16.559 --> 00:22:21.840
The huts have a roof made of straw. It is
supported by a frame to which the clay is

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:28.840
applied in long and consistent strokes. This
is a slow process to ensure that all the nooks

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:33.570
and crannies are filled.

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:39.039
When the appropriate thickness of coat has
been achieved, a process of rubbing and smoothing

00:22:39.039 --> 00:22:40.630
takes place.

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:47.630
At the end, the wall will have the blue shine
of a ceramic glaze. But, even in these houses,

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:55.580
people are exposed to mosquito bites during
the rainy season.

00:22:57.100 --> 00:23:04.100
The journey ends in Wau from which the specimens
will be dispatched to Khartoum for storage.

00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:09.990
Neutralization tests were performed on six
hundred and sixty-six human sera.

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:15.630
They showed that the percentage of yellow
fever positive samples was higher in the south

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:21.320
in the Bar el Arab area. In the age group
of fifteen years and over, the reactors were

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:25.309
eighty percent and thirty percent, respectively.

00:23:25.309 --> 00:23:31.539
The occurrence of positive children and young
primates proved that since 1940, yellow fever

00:23:31.539 --> 00:23:34.820
had become endemic in southern Sudan.

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:39.919
The seasonal shifting of cattle and people
combined with roaming bands of grivet monkeys

00:23:39.919 --> 00:23:45.580
provided all the necessary ingredients for
virus re-introduction and maintenance of the

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:46.909
transmission cycle.

00:23:46.909 --> 00:23:53.909
It is now time to return to El Obeid with
a last glimpse of the wildlife of tropical

00:23:54.460 --> 00:24:01.460
southern Sudan. In Wau, this DC3 is the workhorse
for transport in more remote areas. It will

00:24:04.799 --> 00:24:08.559
bring the cargo of precious specimens to Khartoum.

00:24:08.559 --> 00:24:15.559
There is a last look at Khartoum in its magical
robe of sands and at the Blue Nile which separates

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:21.840
the tropical areas of southern Sudan from
the north and upper Egypt.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:22.110
[End. Original 16mm footage transfered to
digital prior to editing. Copyright: Work

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:22.610
Family Estate and National Library of Medicine,
Historical Collections.]

